  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(u+v)=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u$, $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}\u2061v+{cos}^{2}\u2061v=1\\,\u27f9\\,{sin}^{2}\u2061v=1-{cos}^{2}\u2061v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061v & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{2})+(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4\\sqrt{3}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
